Linux Networking Commands Reference

Project: Networking Fundamentals

Platform: Kali Linux

1. Network Identification

Display hostname

    hostname

Displays the computer’s hostname.

Display network interfaces

    ip link show

Lists available network interfaces and their operational states.

Display IP addresses

    ip addr show

Displays IPv4 and IPv6 addresses assigned to network interfaces.

Display simplified interface information

    ip -br addr

Provides a brief overview of network interfaces and their IP addresses.

2. IP Addressing and Routing

Display routing table

    ip route

Displays network routes and the default gateway.

Inspect route to an external destination

    ip route get 8.8.8.8

Displays the route Linux selects to reach the destination.

3. DNS Investigation

Display DNS configuration

    cat /etc/resolv.conf

Displays configured DNS resolver information.

Resolve a domain name

    getent hosts google.com

Retrieves address information for a hostname through the system’s configured name-service mechanisms.

4. Network Connectivity

Test local gateway

    ping -c 4 192.168.100.1

Sends four ICMP echo requests to the local gateway.

Test external IP connectivity

    ping -c 4 8.8.8.8

Tests communication with an external IP address.

Test domain resolution

    getent hosts google.com

Checks whether the system can resolve the domain.

5. TCP and UDP Inspection

Display listening sockets

    ss -tuln

Displays listening TCP and UDP sockets using numeric addresses and ports.

Display active TCP and UDP connections

    ss -tun

Displays TCP and UDP socket information.

Display listening sockets with process information

    sudo ss -tulpn

Displays listening sockets and associated process information where available.

6. Common Network Services

Search common service definitions

    grep -E '^(ssh|http|https|domain|ftp)[[:space:]]' /etc/services

Displays selected service names and their commonly assigned ports.

7. Nmap Reconnaissance

Verify Nmap installation

    nmap --version

Displays the installed Nmap version and build information.

Scan localhost

    nmap 127.0.0.1

Scans the default 1,000 commonly used TCP ports on the local machine.

Detect service versions

    nmap -sV 127.0.0.1

Attempts to identify services and versions on discovered open ports.

Scan all TCP ports

    nmap -p- 127.0.0.1

Scans TCP ports 1 through 65535.

Scan a specific TCP port

    nmap -p 8000 127.0.0.1

Checks the state of TCP port 8000 on localhost.

8. Local Laboratory Web Server

The following commands are for the planned local web-server experiment.

Start a local Python HTTP server

    python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1

Starts a simple HTTP server bound only to the local loopback interface.

Inspect port 8000

    sudo ss -tulpn | grep 8000

Checks whether a process is listening on port 8000.

Scan the local service

    nmap -sV -p 8000 127.0.0.1

Attempts to identify the service running on port 8000.

Stop the server

Press:

Ctrl + C

9. Command Options Explained

Option	Meaning
-c	Specifies the number of ping requests
-t	Selects TCP sockets
-u	Selects UDP sockets
-l	Displays listening sockets
-n	Uses numeric addresses and ports
-p	Specifies Nmap ports
-p-	Scans all TCP ports
-sV	Attempts service/version detection
-tulpn	Displays listening TCP/UDP sockets and process details

10. Conclusion

This command reference documents the Linux networking utilities and Nmap commands practised during the Networking Fundamentals module.

It serves as a quick reference for future network troubleshooting, network reconnaissance and authorized security assessments.

All scanning activities should be restricted to systems and networks for which permission has been granted.
